.Dd November 3, 2026
.Dt PING 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm ping
.Nd send ICMP and ICMPv6 echo requests
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nq46
.Op Fl c Ar count
.Op Fl i Ar interval
.Op Fl s Ar size
.Op Fl t Ar ttl
.Op Fl W Ar timeout
.Op Fl w Ar deadline
.Ar host
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
sends ICMP echo requests to
.Ar host ,
one a second whether or not the last was answered, and prints each
reply's line, with its size, its sequence number, the hop limit it arrived
with as
.Ql ttl=
and its round-trip time, until
.Ar count
requests are sent and answered, the deadline passes, or an interrupt
comes; it then prints the statistics, in the form Linux's iputils
.Nm
prints them.
Root privilege is not required, and any number of
.Nm
programs run at once, each counting only its own replies.
.Pp
The host may be an IPv4 address, a name, or an IPv6 address.
A link-local IPv6 address, or a group of link scope, may be followed by
.Ql %
and the name or index of the interface it is reached by, as in
.Ql fe80::1%eth0 ,
which a machine with more than one card requires.
Names resolve only to IPv4 addresses.
Names are looked up in
.Pa /etc/hosts ,
or
.Pa /hosts
when that file is absent, then through a DNS A query to the first valid
.Ql nameserver
in
.Pa /etc/resolv.conf ,
or
.Pa /resolv.conf
when that file is absent.
The default DNS server is
.Ql 10.0.2.3 .
The resolver retries for about six seconds unless a matching response
arrives.
.Pp
The requests go through an ICMP datagram socket,
.Fn socket AF_INET SOCK_DGRAM IPPROTO_ICMP
or its IPv6 form, as
.Xr socket 2
describes, which any user may open, as iputils uses it on Linux.
The kernel gives the socket an echo identifier no other socket holds,
writes it into each request with the checksum, and hands the socket only
the replies that carry it.
Each request has 56 bytes of data, 84 with its headers, as iputils sends
by default, or the size of
.Fl s ,
and the hop limit 64 or that of
.Fl t .
.Nm
times each round trip on the monotonic clock, to the microsecond, from
the request's send to its reply, and counts a reply against its own
sequence number however late it comes, while the request is among the
last 4096 sent; a second reply to one request is not counted.
The hop limit of a reply comes from
.Dv IP_RECVTTL ,
or
.Dv IPV6_RECVHOPLIMIT
for IPv6.
.Pp
Once the last request is sent,
.Nm
waits for the replies still out: twice the longest round trip, or one
interval if that is longer, when a reply has come, and otherwise the
timeout of
.Fl W ,
ten seconds by default.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width "-w deadline"
.It Fl c Ar count
Stop after
.Ar count
requests, once their replies have come or the wait for them is over.
.It Fl i Ar interval
Send a request every
.Ar interval
seconds, which may have a fraction, instead of every second.
.It Fl s Ar size
Send
.Ar size
bytes of data in each request, from 0 to 65507, or 65527 for IPv6.
A request past one datagram of the 1500-byte MTU leaves in fragments,
and its reply is reassembled.
.It Fl t Ar ttl
Send each request with the hop limit
.Ar ttl ,
from 1 to 255, instead of 64.
.It Fl W Ar timeout
Wait
.Ar timeout
seconds after the last request when no reply has come at all, instead of
ten; 0 waits until an interrupt.
Once a reply has come, the wait is twice the longest round trip.
.It Fl w Ar deadline
Stop when
.Ar deadline
seconds have passed since the first request, whatever has been sent or
received.
With
.Fl c
as well,
.Nm
does not stop when the count is sent: it waits for the count's replies or
the deadline, whichever comes first.
.It Fl q
Print only the first line and the statistics.
.It Fl 6
Require an IPv6 address.
.It Fl n , Fl 4
Accepted and ignored: replies are printed by address, and a request is
IPv4 unless its address is IPv6.
.El
.Pp
For IPv6 the first line has the form iputils gives it,
.Ql PING ::1(::1) 56 data bytes .
The kernel resolves no IPv6 neighbour yet, so only ::1, this machine's
own addresses and groups answer; a unicast address on another host
fails with
.Ql ping: sendmsg: Host is unreachable .
.Pp
On os-real, this program is installed as
.Pa /bin/ping .
BusyBox's ping applet is not built.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /etc/resolv.conf
.It Pa /etc/hosts
Host names and addresses.
.It Pa /etc/resolv.conf
DNS server configuration.
.It Pa /hosts , /resolv.conf
The files read when the two above are absent.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
returns 0 when a reply arrived, and with both
.Fl c
and
.Fl w ,
when
.Ar count
replies arrived.
It returns 1 when they did not, and 2 for an option it does not take, a
count or a time that is not a number, a host it cannot resolve, and a
socket or first request the kernel refuses.
.Sh EXAMPLES
.Dl ping -c 3 192.168.0.1
.Dl ping -c 2 -W 1 github.com
.Dl ping -q -w 5 1.1.1.1
.Dl ping -6 -c 2 ::1
.Dl ping -c 2 ff02::1%eth0
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql ping: HOST: cannot resolve the name
The host is neither an address nor a name the files or DNS answer for.
.It Ql ping: HOST: not an IPv6 address
.Fl 6
was given with a host that is not an IPv6 address.
.It Ql ping: NAME: no such interface
The interface after
.Ql %
is neither an index nor the name of an interface.
.It Ql ping: socket: MESSAGE
The kernel would not open the ICMP datagram socket, for the reason
.Xr strerror 3
gives.
.It Ql ping: sendmsg: MESSAGE
The kernel could not send a request, for the reason
.Xr strerror 3
gives.
.Nm
stops when it is the first request, and otherwise counts the request as
sent and goes on.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr busybox-networking 1 ,
.Xr nc 1 ,
.Xr socket 2
